    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Antiarrhythmics: Vaughan-Williams Classification",
     "content": "Cardiac arrhythmias range from benign palpitations to fatal ventricular fibrillation. The **Vaughan-Williams classification** organizes antiarrhythmic drugs by their primary mechanism of action on cardiac ion channels.\n\n**Class I — Sodium Channel Blockers** (subdivided by how strongly they block):\n• **Ia**: Procainamide, quinidine — moderate Na+ block, prolong repolarization\n• **Ib**: Lidocaine — fast Na+ block, used in ventricular arrhythmias (also a local anesthetic)\n• **Ic**: Flecainide — strong Na+ block, for supraventricular tachycardias\n\n**Class II — Beta-Blockers**:\n• Metoprolol, atenolol, esmolol (ultra-short acting IV)\n• Slow SA and AV node conduction\n• First-line for rate control in atrial fibrillation\n\n**Class III — Potassium Channel Blockers** (prolong repolarization):\n• **Amiodarone** — the \"dirty drug\" that hits all four classes; used for life-threatening arrhythmias but has severe toxicities (thyroid, pulmonary fibrosis, liver, corneal deposits)\n• **Sotalol** — also has beta-blocking properties\n• **Dofetilide** — requires inpatient initiation due to torsades de pointes risk\n\n**Class IV — Calcium Channel Blockers**:\n• **Verapamil**, **diltiazem** — slow AV conduction\n• Used for supraventricular tachycardias and rate control in a-fib\n\n**Unclassified**: **Adenosine** — first-line for acute SVT (6mg rapid IV push); **digoxin** — rate control in a-fib.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">VAUGHAN-WILLIAMS CLASSIFICATION</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Four classes of antiarrhythmic drugs by ion channel</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"130\" width=\"480\" height=\"230\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">CLASS I — Na+ CHANNEL BLOCKERS</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"16\">Ia: Procainamide, Quinidine</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Moderate block, prolong repolarization</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"16\">Ib: Lidocaine</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Fast block, ventricular arrhythmias</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"16\">Ic: Flecainide</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Strong block, SVT</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"130\" width=\"470\" height=\"230\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"805\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">CLASS II — BETA-BLOCKERS</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Metoprolol, Atenolol</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Esmolol (ultra-short IV)</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Slow SA and AV node conduction</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"315\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">First-line for rate control</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"345\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">in atrial fibrillation</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"390\" width=\"480\" height=\"260\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">CLASS III — K+ CHANNEL BLOCKERS</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Prolong repolarization</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"505\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Amiodarone — the 'dirty drug'</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Hits all 4 classes. Life-threatening arrhythmias</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"560\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Toxicities: thyroid, lung, liver, cornea</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"595\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Sotalol — also a beta-blocker</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Dofetilide — inpatient initiation only</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"390\" width=\"470\" height=\"260\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"805\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">CLASS IV — Ca++ CHANNEL BLOCKERS</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"470\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Verapamil, Diltiazem</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"505\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">Slow AV conduction</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">SVT and a-fib rate control</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"580\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"18\">UNCLASSIFIED</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"610\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Adenosine — acute SVT (rapid IV push)</text><text x=\"805\" y=\"640\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Digoxin — rate control in a-fib</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"690\" width=\"800\" height=\"80\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"740\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"19\">Mnemonic: \"Some Block Potassium Channels\" — Na+, Beta, K+, Ca++</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Vaughan-Williams classification of antiarrhythmic drugs"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Antilipemics: Statins and Beyond",
     "content": "High cholesterol — specifically elevated **LDL** (\"bad\" cholesterol) — is a major modifiable risk factor for atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. Antilipemic drugs are among the most widely prescribed medications worldwide.\n\n**Statins** (the \"-statin\" drugs) — the gold standard:\n• Mechanism: inhibit **HMG-CoA reductase**, the rate-limiting enzyme in hepatic cholesterol synthesis\n• The liver compensates by upregulating LDL receptors, pulling more LDL out of the blood\n• **Atorvastatin** (Lipitor) — high-intensity, one of the best-selling drugs in history\n• **Rosuvastatin** (Crestor) — highest potency per milligram\n• **Simvastatin** (Zocor) — moderate intensity, many drug interactions\n• Side effects: **myopathy** (muscle pain), rhabdomyolysis (rare but serious), liver enzyme elevation\n• Monitor: lipid panel, liver function tests, CK if muscle symptoms develop\n\n**Beyond statins:**\n• **Ezetimibe** (Zetia) — blocks cholesterol absorption in the intestine, often added to a statin\n• **Fibrates** (gemfibrozil, fenofibrate) — primarily lower **triglycerides**\n• **PCSK9 inhibitors** (evolocumab, alirocumab) — injectable monoclonal antibodies that dramatically lower LDL; reserved for familial hypercholesterolemia or statin intolerance\n• **Bile acid sequestrants** (cholestyramine) — bind bile acids, forcing liver to use cholesterol to make more\n• **Omega-3 fatty acids** (icosapent ethyl/Vascepa) — FDA-approved for triglyceride reduction",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">ANTILIPEMIC AGENTS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Lowering cholesterol to prevent heart disease</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"130\" width=\"940\" height=\"300\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">STATINS (\"-statin\") — GOLD STANDARD</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Mechanism: Inhibit HMG-CoA Reductase → ↓ hepatic cholesterol → ↑ LDL receptors</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"230\" width=\"250\" height=\"170\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.08)\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">HIGH INTENSITY</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Atorvastatin 40-80mg</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Rosuvastatin 20-40mg</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"355\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">↓ LDL by ≥50%</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Post-MI standard</text><rect x=\"400\" y=\"230\" width=\"250\" height=\"170\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.08)\"/><text x=\"525\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">MODERATE</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Atorvastatin 10-20mg</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Simvastatin 20-40mg</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"355\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">↓ LDL by 30-49%</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Primary prevention</text><rect x=\"680\" y=\"230\" width=\"300\" height=\"170\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.08)\"/><text x=\"830\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">SIDE EFFECTS</text><text x=\"830\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Myopathy (muscle pain)</text><text x=\"830\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Rhabdomyolysis (rare)</text><text x=\"830\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">↑ Liver enzymes</text><text x=\"830\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Monitor LFTs, CK</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"470\" width=\"300\" height=\"180\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"18\">EZETIMIBE</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"545\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Blocks intestinal</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"570\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">cholesterol absorption</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"605\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Add-on to statin therapy</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"630\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Zetia brand</text><rect x=\"410\" y=\"470\" width=\"280\" height=\"180\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"18\">FIBRATES</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"545\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Gemfibrozil, Fenofibrate</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"575\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Lower TRIGLYCERIDES</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"610\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Not primarily for LDL</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"635\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Myopathy risk with statins</text><rect x=\"720\" y=\"470\" width=\"300\" height=\"180\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(236,72,153,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"870\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"18\">PCSK9 INHIBITORS</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"545\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Evolocumab, Alirocumab</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"575\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Injectable monoclonals</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"610\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Dramatic LDL reduction</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"635\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Very expensive</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Statin mechanism and non-statin antilipemic agents"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "visual",
     "title": "Digoxin and Nitroglycerin: High-Alert Medications",
     "content": "Two cardiovascular drugs deserve special attention because of their **narrow therapeutic indices** — meaning the difference between a therapeutic dose and a toxic dose is dangerously small.\n\n**Digoxin** (Lanoxin) — a cardiac glycoside:\n• Mechanism: inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase pump → increases intracellular calcium → **increases cardiac contractility** (positive inotrope) and **slows heart rate** (negative chronotrope)\n• Used for: heart failure (improves symptoms) and rate control in atrial fibrillation\n• Therapeutic level: **0.5-2.0 ng/mL** — toxicity can be fatal\n• Toxicity signs: nausea/vomiting, visual disturbances (yellow-green halos), arrhythmias\n• **Hypokalemia increases toxicity risk** — critical when patient is also on a diuretic\n• Reversal: **digoxin immune Fab** (Digibind)\n\n**Nitroglycerin (NTG)** — the classic angina drug:\n• Mechanism: releases **nitric oxide (NO)**, causing venous dilation → reduces preload → decreases myocardial oxygen demand\n• Routes: sublingual tablet, spray, transdermal patch, IV drip\n• Sublingual onset: **1-3 minutes** — \"take one tablet, wait 5 minutes, take another if needed, call 911 after three doses\"\n• Key issue: **tolerance** — must have a 10-12 hour \"nitrate-free interval\" daily\n• Contraindicated with PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil/Viagra) — **fatal hypotension**",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">HIGH-ALERT CV MEDICATIONS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Narrow therapeutic index drugs require extra vigilance</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"130\" width=\"980\" height=\"400\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"175\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">DIGOXIN (Lanoxin)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Cardiac glycoside — inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase → ↑ contractility, ↓ heart rate</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"240\" width=\"290\" height=\"130\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.08)\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"275\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">THERAPEUTIC RANGE</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">0.5 - 2.0 ng/mL</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"345\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"15\">Very narrow window!</text><rect x=\"410\" y=\"240\" width=\"290\" height=\"130\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.08)\"/><text x=\"555\" y=\"275\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">TOXICITY SIGNS</text><text x=\"555\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Nausea, vomiting</text><text x=\"555\" y=\"335\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Yellow-green halos</text><text x=\"555\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Arrhythmias</text><rect x=\"720\" y=\"240\" width=\"290\" height=\"130\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.08)\"/><text x=\"865\" y=\"275\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">KEY INTERACTIONS</text><text x=\"865\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Hypokalemia ↑ toxicity</text><text x=\"865\" y=\"335\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">(diuretic + digoxin = danger)</text><text x=\"865\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Reversal: Digibind</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Used for: Heart failure (↑ contractility) and A-fib (rate control)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"455\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Positive inotrope + negative chronotrope + negative dromotrope</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Always check potassium before giving digoxin</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"560\" width=\"980\" height=\"350\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"605\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">NITROGLYCERIN (NTG)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"640\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Releases nitric oxide → venous dilation → ↓ preload → ↓ O2 demand</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"670\" width=\"290\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.08)\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"705\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">SUBLINGUAL</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"735\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Onset: 1-3 minutes</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"760\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">3 doses max, then call 911</text><rect x=\"410\" y=\"670\" width=\"290\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.08)\"/><text x=\"555\" y=\"705\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">TOLERANCE</text><text x=\"555\" y=\"735\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Must have 10-12hr</text><text x=\"555\" y=\"760\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">nitrate-free interval</text><rect x=\"720\" y=\"670\" width=\"290\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.08)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"865\" y=\"705\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">DEADLY COMBO</text><text x=\"865\" y=\"735\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">NTG + Viagra = fatal</text><text x=\"865\" y=\"760\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"14\">hypotension</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"850\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Routes: sublingual tab, spray, transdermal patch, IV drip</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"885\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Store sublingual tablets in dark glass; replace every 6 months</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Digoxin and nitroglycerin: narrow therapeutic index drugs"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Polypharmacy in Cardiovascular Disease",
     "content": "**Real-world scenario:** Mr. Davis, 68, presents with hypertension, atrial fibrillation, heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF), hyperlipidemia, and type 2 diabetes. His medication list reads like a cardiovascular pharmacology textbook:\n\n• **Carvedilol** 25mg BID — beta-blocker for HFrEF + rate control for a-fib\n• **Lisinopril** 20mg daily — ACE inhibitor for HFrEF + HTN\n• **Spironolactone** 25mg daily — aldosterone antagonist for HFrEF\n• **Furosemide** 40mg daily — loop diuretic for fluid management\n• **Apixaban** 5mg BID — DOAC for stroke prevention in a-fib\n• **Atorvastatin** 80mg daily — high-intensity statin for hyperlipidemia\n• **Metformin** 1000mg BID — for diabetes\n\nThat's **seven** cardiovascular and metabolic drugs, and this is a completely standard regimen. HIM professionals must understand why each drug is there — it informs code assignment, validates medical necessity, and helps identify potential query opportunities when documentation is incomplete.\n\nNotice the potassium balance: furosemide wastes potassium while spironolactone spares it. This intentional pairing is common and worth recognizing.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">CV POLYPHARMACY CASE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Mr. Davis, 68 — 7 medications, all justified</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"130\" width=\"490\" height=\"100\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"305\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"18\">DIAGNOSES</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">HTN | A-fib | HFrEF | Hyperlipidemia | T2DM</text><rect x=\"560\" y=\"130\" width=\"480\" height=\"100\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"18\">ICD-10 CODES</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">I10 | I48.91 | I50.22 | E78.5 | E11.9</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"260\" width=\"490\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.08)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"305\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Carvedilol 25mg BID</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Beta-blocker → HFrEF + A-fib rate control</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"360\" width=\"490\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.08)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"305\" y=\"395\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Lisinopril 20mg daily</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"425\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">ACE inhibitor → HFrEF + HTN</text><rect x=\"560\" y=\"260\" width=\"480\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.08)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Spironolactone 25mg daily</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Aldosterone antagonist → HFrEF (K+ sparing)</text><rect x=\"560\" y=\"360\" width=\"480\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.08)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"395\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Furosemide 40mg daily</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"425\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Loop diuretic → fluid management (K+ wasting)</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"460\" width=\"490\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.08)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"305\" y=\"495\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Apixaban 5mg BID</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"525\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">DOAC → stroke prevention in A-fib</text><rect x=\"560\" y=\"460\" width=\"480\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.08)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"495\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Atorvastatin 80mg daily</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"525\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">High-intensity statin → hyperlipidemia</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"560\" width=\"980\" height=\"90\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.08)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"595\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">Metformin 1000mg BID</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Biguanide → Type 2 diabetes management</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"690\" width=\"800\" height=\"100\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"725\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"18\">POTASSIUM BALANCE TRICK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"760\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"16\">Furosemide WASTES K+ | Spironolactone SPARES K+ — intentional pairing</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Typical cardiovascular polypharmacy case with coding implications"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "application",
     "title": "HIM Coding for Cardiovascular Medications",
     "content": "Cardiovascular medications generate more coding work than any other drug class. Here's what you need to know for real-world HIM practice:\n\n**Z79 codes for long-term drug use** are critical:\n• Z79.01 — Long-term use of anticoagulants (warfarin, DOACs)\n• Z79.02 — Long-term use of antithrombotics/antiplatelets (aspirin, clopidogrel)\n• Z79.899 — Other long-term drug therapy (statins, antihypertensives)\n\n**Adverse effects vs. underdosing:**\n• Patient takes warfarin correctly, INR goes too high → **adverse effect** (T45.515_)\n• Patient misses warfarin doses, develops a clot → **underdosing** (T45.516_)\n• Someone intentionally gives too much warfarin → **poisoning** (T45.511_ to T45.514_)\n\n**Query opportunities** — when you should ask the physician for clarification:\n• Patient on two antihypertensives but no hypertension diagnosis documented\n• Anticoagulant prescribed without documented indication (a-fib? DVT? PE?)\n• Statin therapy without hyperlipidemia or ASCVD documentation\n• Heart failure type not specified (HFrEF vs. HFpEF affects MS-DRG)\n\nUnderstanding **why** each drug is prescribed lets you validate medical necessity and catch documentation gaps.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">HIM CODING FOR CV DRUGS</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"110\" width=\"980\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">Z79 LONG-TERM DRUG USE CODES</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Z79.01 — Anticoagulants (warfarin, DOACs)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"220\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Z79.02 — Antithrombotics/Antiplatelets (aspirin, clopidogrel)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Z79.899 — Other long-term therapy (statins, antihypertensives)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">These codes capture ongoing medication use and affect risk adjustment</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"360\" width=\"980\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">T-CODE DISTINCTION (Critical for HIM!)</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"430\" width=\"290\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.08)\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"16\">ADVERSE EFFECT</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"490\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"14\">Took correctly, bad result</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"515\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"14\">T45.515_</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"540\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"13\">INR too high on warfarin</text><rect x=\"410\" y=\"430\" width=\"290\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.08)\"/><text x=\"555\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"16\">UNDERDOSING</text><text x=\"555\" y=\"490\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"14\">Missed doses, bad result</text><text x=\"555\" y=\"515\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"14\">T45.516_</text><text x=\"555\" y=\"540\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"13\">Clot from missed doses</text><rect x=\"720\" y=\"430\" width=\"290\" height=\"120\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.08)\"/><text x=\"865\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"16\">POISONING</text><text x=\"865\" y=\"490\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"14\">Intentional/accidental OD</text><text x=\"865\" y=\"515\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"14\">T45.511_ - T45.514_</text><text x=\"865\" y=\"540\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"13\">Intent determines 5th char</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"610\" width=\"980\" height=\"230\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"650\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">QUERY OPPORTUNITIES</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"690\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">• 2 antihypertensives but no HTN diagnosis documented</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"720\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">• Anticoagulant without indication (A-fib? DVT? PE?)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"750\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">• Statin without hyperlipidemia or ASCVD documented</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"780\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">• Heart failure type unspecified (HFrEF vs HFpEF affects DRG)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"815\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Understanding drugs helps you catch documentation gaps</text></svg>",
      "caption": "HIM coding considerations for cardiovascular medications"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "A patient is on digoxin for heart failure and is also taking furosemide for fluid overload. Lab results show potassium of 3.1 mEq/L (low). Why is this combination particularly dangerous?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Furosemide makes digoxin less effective, requiring higher doses",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Hypokalemia increases the risk of digoxin toxicity, which can cause fatal arrhythmias",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "The two drugs compete for the same renal excretion pathway",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Furosemide converts digoxin into a toxic metabolite in the liver",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "Digoxin and potassium compete for the same binding site on the Na+/K+ ATPase pump. When potassium is low (hypokalemia), more digoxin binds to the pump, amplifying its effects to toxic levels. Furosemide is a loop diuretic that wastes potassium, making this a classic dangerous drug interaction. This is why patients on both drugs require regular potassium monitoring — and why spironolactone is often added to the regimen.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">DIGOXIN + HYPOKALEMIA = DANGER</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"120\" width=\"400\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">NORMAL K+ (3.5-5.0)</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">K+ and digoxin compete</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">for Na+/K+ ATPase</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">binding site</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"16\">Balanced → therapeutic effect</text><rect x=\"600\" y=\"120\" width=\"400\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">LOW K+ (&lt;3.5)</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Less K+ competing</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">= more digoxin binds</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">= TOXICITY</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"16\">Arrhythmias, death</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"370\" width=\"800\" height=\"150\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"415\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">THE DANGEROUS CHAIN</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"455\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Furosemide (loop diuretic) → K+ wasted in urine → hypokalemia</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"485\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">→ digoxin toxicity risk skyrockets → arrhythmias</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"505\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">This is why K+ must be monitored in every patient on both drugs</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"560\" width=\"700\" height=\"100\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"600\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"20\">CLINICAL SOLUTION</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"635\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Add spironolactone (K+ sparing) to balance the K+ loss</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Why hypokalemia makes digoxin toxicity more likely"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Antiplatelet Agents and Thrombolytics",
     "content": "Beyond anticoagulants, two more drug categories fight dangerous clots — and understanding the difference is essential for accurate coding.\n\n**Antiplatelet agents** prevent platelet aggregation (the initial step of clot formation):\n• **Aspirin** — irreversibly inhibits **COX-1**, blocking thromboxane A2 production. Low-dose (81mg) for prevention; higher doses for acute events\n• **Clopidogrel** (Plavix) — inhibits **ADP receptor P2Y12** on platelets. Standard after coronary stent placement\n• **Prasugrel** (Effient), **ticagrelor** (Brilinta) — newer, more potent P2Y12 inhibitors\n• **Dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT)**: aspirin + a P2Y12 inhibitor — standard after acute coronary syndrome or stent placement, typically for 12 months\n\n**Thrombolytics** (\"clot busters\") — dissolve existing clots in emergencies:\n• **Alteplase** (tPA) — tissue plasminogen activator, used in acute ischemic stroke (within 4.5 hours of onset) and massive PE\n• **Tenecteplase** — modified tPA, single bolus dosing\n• Major risk: **hemorrhage** — these drugs don't distinguish between dangerous clots and beneficial ones (surgical sites, brain)\n\n**Coding distinction:**\n• Anticoagulants → Z79.01\n• Antiplatelets → Z79.02\n• These are NOT interchangeable codes — getting this right matters for risk adjustment and quality measures.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">ANTIPLATELETS &amp; THROMBOLYTICS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Preventing vs. dissolving clots</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"130\" width=\"980\" height=\"340\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">ANTIPLATELET AGENTS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Prevent platelet aggregation — the first step of clot formation</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"230\" width=\"300\" height=\"210\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.08)\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">ASPIRIN</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">Irreversible COX-1 inhibitor</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">Blocks thromboxane A2</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"355\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">81mg = prevention</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">325mg = acute event</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"415\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Cheap, proven, universal</text><rect x=\"420\" y=\"230\" width=\"300\" height=\"210\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.08)\"/><text x=\"570\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">P2Y12 INHIBITORS</text><text x=\"570\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">Clopidogrel (Plavix)</text><text x=\"570\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">Prasugrel (Effient)</text><text x=\"570\" y=\"345\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">Ticagrelor (Brilinta)</text><text x=\"570\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Block ADP receptor</text><text x=\"570\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">on platelets</text><text x=\"570\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Standard after stent</text><rect x=\"740\" y=\"230\" width=\"260\" height=\"210\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.08)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"870\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">DAPT</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">Aspirin + P2Y12</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"330\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"15\">inhibitor together</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"370\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">After ACS or stent</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"395\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Usually 12 months</text><text x=\"870\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"14\">Bleeding risk higher</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"510\" width=\"980\" height=\"230\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"550\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">THROMBOLYTICS — 'CLOT BUSTERS'</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"585\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">Dissolve EXISTING clots in emergencies</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"630\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Alteplase (tPA) — ischemic stroke, PE</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"660\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Tenecteplase — single bolus form</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"630\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"17\">MAJOR RISK</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"660\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Hemorrhage — no clot</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"690\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">selectivity</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"720\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">Stroke window: must give within 4.5 hours of symptom onset</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"770\" width=\"700\" height=\"90\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"805\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"18\">CODING: Anticoagulants = Z79.01 | Antiplatelets = Z79.02</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"835\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"15\">NOT interchangeable — different Z codes for different drug classes</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Antiplatelets vs thrombolytics: mechanisms and coding"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "Which of the following correctly pairs a cardiovascular drug with its primary mechanism of action?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Atorvastatin — blocks calcium channels to lower cholesterol",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Amiodarone — inhibits HMG-CoA reductase in the liver",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Furosemide — blocks Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the loop of Henle",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Warfarin — directly inhibits factor Xa in the clotting cascade",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "Furosemide is a **loop diuretic** that works by blocking the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the ascending loop of Henle, preventing sodium and water reabsorption. Atorvastatin inhibits HMG-CoA reductase (not calcium channels). Amiodarone is a Class III antiarrhythmic (potassium channel blocker). Warfarin is a vitamin K antagonist, not a direct factor Xa inhibitor — that describes apixaban and rivaroxaban (DOACs).",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"34\">DRUG-MECHANISM MATCHING</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Know the mechanism, know the drug</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"140\" width=\"460\" height=\"130\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"20\">FUROSEMIDE (Lasix)</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Loop diuretic</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Blocks Na+/K+/2Cl- in loop of Henle</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"140\" width=\"460\" height=\"130\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"20\">ATORVASTATIN (Lipitor)</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">NOT a calcium channel blocker</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"300\" width=\"460\" height=\"130\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"20\">AMIODARONE</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Class III antiarrhythmic</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">K+ channel blocker (+ all classes)</text><rect x=\"570\" y=\"300\" width=\"460\" height=\"130\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"20\">WARFARIN (Coumadin)</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Vitamin K antagonist</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">NOT a direct Xa inhibitor</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"480\" width=\"700\" height=\"100\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"520\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"20\">COMMON EXAM TRAP</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"555\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"17\">Warfarin ≠ DOACs: warfarin blocks vitamin K, DOACs block factor Xa or thrombin</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Matching cardiovascular drugs to their mechanisms"
     }
    }
